​ @justjack8989  Which is wrong. There are other plattforms with ReBar as well (like LGA2066) and that was not my point anyways. Intel 10. and 11. Gen are usually running on PCIe 3.0 (excluding B760 and Z590, which are rare). And Ryzen 3000 happily runs on 300 and 400 chipssets, which are only PCIe 3.0. All those support ReBar, but the x8 interface on all the above mentoined cards /RTX 4060/TI, RX7600/X and now B580) are seriously bottlenecked by 3.0. If any of them would really want build a decent low budget card for upgrading older systems they would give them an x16 interface. AMD and Nvidia fucked it up and now Intel does as well. They were doing so well until this point...
Back to used A750, RX6600 and RTX3060 for upgrades...

And since my Answers keep getting not posted for some reason, I add the answers here:

@Igor369 It is a private PC workshop. So, usually testing hardware, cloning drives, testing differents OSs (like Linux distros, etc). I build a few PCs a months for ppl (mostly kids and students) with usually a low budget. So I am always on the lookout for affordable hardware with a good price to performance ratio. So I bought the A750 as a test subject (and out of curiousity). I run regular game-benchmarks on it to see the performance. And even with the old i5 10400, 32 GB of slow DDR4-2667 and an Samsung 950 Pro, it is an excellent 1080p card. And you could get in for around 150 € used, which is excellent value for money. 

@Frizzy9000: I just googled it again, just to be sure. German site PC Welt tested a RTX 4090 and a 4060 TI on a PCIe 4.0 vs 3.0 interface. The big boy had less issues, only around 5-8% loss on the slower interface. 16 lanes are 16 lanes. The downgrade on the small one was significant. They said going from PCIe 4.0 to 3.0 was like downgrading from a 4060 TI to a 3060 TI. And the chipmaker do know that. And ARC needs ReBar to work properly and ReBar needs bandwith. No image what happens if you plug this x8 card in a 3.0 slot. AMD 7600/X is not better.

