@mateusbmedeiros  Yeah, really weird indeed. Nvidia would need Intel to license it to them, which is really unlikely to happen. And then AMD would need to license them the x64 extension. That's why the x86 market is such a bad duopoly.
Some possibilities may be if both intel and AMD become bankrupt (and then maybe their architecture can be freely used), or if Nvidia buys either of them. Or maybe if some government intervenes in the licensing and makes it readily available to end the duopoly.
Of course, this is all about x86_64. If computers really shift to ARM, the possibilities are endless.

 @WWIA7062-2  This just shows how insanely exaggerated the reporting got. There was only ever a precious few reports of 14700Ks becoming unstable. Well below expected failure rates for any CPU with millions sold. 99.9% of verifiable accounts were i9s, and only K sku parts. 

It was only theoretical that it could effect all k skus which quickly turned into "all 13th and 14th gen will self explode!!1!!" because we had a slow summer and news outlets needed the clickbait so it was laughably exaggerated and sensationalized. Notice how quickly and magically the problem completely vanished once Intel released a fix and outlets had new news to report? Shouldn't people that don't follow tech news still have their computers failing left and right? No, because it was already ridiculously exaggerated to begin with.

Regardless, on the budget front all non-k i5s and i3s are pretty much identical all through 12th-14th gen so you're best off getting a 12th gen since they're the cheapest. (It's barely more for a 12600k than 12-13-14100s and it's a LOT faster chip.)
